Regression with XGBoost & Model Evaluation
Extreme Gradient Boosting with XGBoost course! In this chapter, we transition from classification to regression tasks, explore different types of base learners, evaluate models using cross-validation metrics, and dive into model visualization and tuning.

📌 Key Topics & Concepts Covered
1. Trees as Base Learners
XGBoost defaults to using decision trees as base learners (booster="gbtree").

You can build regression models using xgb.XGBRegressor to predict continuous target variables (such as housing prices in Ames, Iowa).

2. Linear Base Learners
XGBoost also supports linear base learners (booster="gblinear"), allowing you to create regularized linear regressions using the powerful learning API.

These require using the non-scikit-learn compatible functions like xgb.train() and converting data into xgb.DMatrix objects.

3. Evaluating Model Quality
Metrics: Use metrics="rmse" (Root Mean Squared Error) or metrics="mae" (Mean Absolute Error) inside xgb.cv() to assess out-of-sample model performance.

Regularization: Control model complexity and prevent overfitting by tuning L2 regularization (lambda) and L1 regularization (alpha) parameters.

4. Model Visualization & Interpretability
Individual Trees: Use xgb.plot_tree() with the num_trees parameter (0-indexed) and rankdir="LR" to visually explore how decisions are made across boosting rounds.

Feature Importance: Use xgb.plot_importance() to rank features based on how frequently they are split across all trees in the model.

5. Hyperparameter Tuning & Early Stopping
Boosting Rounds: Tuning the number of boosting rounds (num_boost_round) directly impacts performance.

Early Stopping: Use the early_stopping_rounds parameter in xgb.cv() to automatically halt training when the evaluation metric stops improving, saving computation time and preventing overfitting.

🚀 Quick Code Reference
Training a Tree Regressor
Python
import xgboost as xgb
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error
import numpy as np

# Instantiate and fit regressor
xg_reg = xgb.XGBRegressor(seed=123, objective="reg:squarederror", n_estimators=10)
xg_reg.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Predict and evaluate
preds = xg_reg.predict(X_test)
rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_test, preds))
Cross-Validation with Early Stopping
Python
housing_dmatrix = xgb.DMatrix(data=X, label=y)
params = {"objective": "reg:squarederror", "max_depth": 4}

cv_results = xgb.cv(
    dtrain=housing_dmatrix, 
    params=params, 
    nfold=3, 
    num_boost_round=50, 
    early_stopping_rounds=10, 
    metrics="rmse", 
    as_pandas=True, 
    seed=123
)
